course: dictionary
lesson: 5279
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A jug is a container with a handle and a spout, a little lip for pouring. You use it for water, milk or juice. Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant.
explain: Read «the March Hare had upset the milk-jug into his plate». Something was knocked over and spilled into a plate, so the milk-jug is the container that held the milk. Ask yourself what the jug is doing, and the sentence will give you the answer.
explain: Jug can also mean the amount a jug holds, as in «a jug of lemonade». The words of and lemonade show you mean a full jug's worth. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: A jug has a handle and a spout, a lip for pouring liquids.
fact: Jug can mean the container itself, or the amount it holds: «a jug of lemonade».
fact: A measuring jug has lines on its side to show how much liquid is inside.
fact: In science, a measuring jug measures volume in millilitres.
fact: A jug of hot water must be carried carefully, because it can scald you if it spills.
exercise: «The March Hare had upset the milk-jug into his plate.» What is a milk-jug? | A bowl for cereal | A small cup for tea | A plate with a handle | *A container for pouring milk
exercise: A measuring jug has lines on its side. What are the lines for? | To make it look pretty | To stop it sliding off the table | *To show how much liquid is inside | To hold the handle
exercise: In «a jug of lemonade», what does jug mean? | A party game | A glass with no handle | A type of fruit | *The amount a jug holds
test: What does a measuring jug measure? | *Volume of liquid, in millilitres | Weight, in kilograms | Length, in metres | Time, in minutes
test: Why must a jug of hot water be carried carefully? | *Because it can scald you if it spills | Because hot water freezes quickly | Because jugs are as heavy as rocks | Because jugs can fly off the table
exam: Which sentence uses jug to mean the container itself? | A jug of lemonade costs two pounds. | She bought a jug of milk for the party. | *She poured the milk from the jug.
